# Browser Mermaid preview

A diagram can explain structure at a glance, but only if the reader can see it
while reading the explanation. Literate therefore turns an ordinary Mermaid code
fence into a diagram inside VS Code's Markdown preview. The Markdown source stays
visible and editable in the document; the preview supplies the visual companion.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Source[Mermaid fence in the book] --> Container[Safe preview container]
    Container --> Renderer[Locally bundled Mermaid renderer]
    Renderer --> Diagram[SVG shown beside the source]
```

No renderer is fetched from the internet and no other extension is trusted to
provide one. Literate bundles Mermaid itself, which makes the preview reproducible
and prevents a document from silently selecting a different runtime. VS Code
supplies the Electron and Chromium baseline needed to run that local renderer.

This chapter concerns the immediate browser preview only. Static SVG files for
publication travel through a separate, explicitly asynchronous adapter. The pure
generation engine does not open a browser, and reading or generating a project
must not acquire that side effect accidentally.

The preview program is ordinary browser JavaScript bundled locally as an IIFE.
Keeping it outside the extension's TypeScript compilation avoids introducing DOM
and Mermaid dependency types into the Node extension. Its readable source remains
a generated program file; the minified browser bundle is a later build artifact.
The mechanical root that assembles this file is collected in the
[program assembly appendix](assembly.html).

A weak map ties retained source to the actual container, not its source line or
text. Replacing a Markdown preview therefore creates fresh work even for an
identical diagram. IDs use monotonic container and attempt numbers and avoid
existing DOM IDs, including IDs from other preview plugins.

```js : <<Mermaid preview state>>=
const selector = 'pre.literate-mermaid';
const states = new WeakMap();
let nextContainer = 0;
let pending = false;
let running = false;
```

Each render attempt gets a fresh diagram ID. Check both the SVG ID and
Mermaid's temporary wrapper ID before using it.

```js : <<Mermaid preview state>>=+
function diagramId(state) {
    let id;
    do {
        id = `literate-mermaid-${state.container}-${++state.attempt}`;
    } while (document.getElementById(id) || document.getElementById(`d${id}`));
    return id;
}
```

Security and resource limits are fixed by the client. No icons, external fonts,
assets or event bindings are registered. Strict Mermaid SVG is the only HTML
inserted. Disallowing all directives and YAML frontmatter is intentionally more
conservative than relying on Mermaid's list of protected configuration keys:
per-diagram settings must not enable loose rendering or disable limits. This
also means otherwise harmless title/config frontmatter is unsupported.
Initialize once immediately, even before DOM readiness or with no diagrams, to
disable Mermaid's automatic generic `.mermaid` scan. Subsequent initialization
is confined to the serial render queue.

```js : <<Mermaid preview configuration>>=
function theme() {
    const wrapper = document.querySelector('.literate-document');
    return getTheme(wrapper?.getAttribute('data-literate-theme'))?.name || defaultThemeName;
}

function applyTheme() {
    let sheet = document.getElementById('literate-theme-style');
    if (!sheet) {
        sheet = document.createElement('style');
        sheet.id = 'literate-theme-style';
        document.head.appendChild(sheet);
    }
    const css = themeCss(getTheme(theme()));
    if (sheet.textContent !== css) { sheet.textContent = css; }
}
```

The wrapper's catalogue name selects both the scoped document stylesheet and
Mermaid palette, independently of VS Code's theme. Missing or invalid names use
Rose Pine Dawn. Only trusted catalogue CSS is installed, with no body styling;
the single stylesheet is applied even when the document contains no diagrams.

Only the selected theme varies between configurations. Security settings,
resource limits and local font defaults remain fixed for every render.

```js : <<Mermaid preview configuration>>=+
function configuration(currentTheme) {
    return {
        startOnLoad: false,
        securityLevel: 'strict',
        suppressErrorRendering: true,
        maxTextSize: 50000,
        maxEdges: 500,
        ...mermaidTheme(getTheme(currentTheme)),
        fontFamily: 'sans-serif',
        htmlLabels: false,
        flowchart: { htmlLabels: false },
        secure: ['secure', 'securityLevel', 'startOnLoad', 'suppressErrorRendering',
            'maxTextSize', 'maxEdges', 'theme', 'themeVariables', 'fontFamily', 'htmlLabels', 'flowchart']
    };
}

mermaid.initialize(configuration(defaultThemeName));
```

Reject oversized source and diagram-level configuration before invoking
Mermaid, rather than allowing a diagram to alter the preview policy.

```js : <<Mermaid preview configuration>>=+
function validateSource(source) {
    if (source.length > 50000) { throw new Error('Diagram exceeds the 50000 character limit.'); }
    if (/%%\s*\{/.test(source) || /^\s*---(?:\s|$)/.test(source)) {
        throw new Error('Mermaid directives and frontmatter are disabled in this preview.');
    }
}
```

The last installed markup distinguishes our own SVG/error text from an in-place
Markdown source update. Original source never comes from rendered SVG. Failed
attempts are cached too, so repeated update events do not continually retry a
syntax error; changing source or theme permits recovery. Classes and source-line
attributes remain on the original pre element.

```js : <<Mermaid preview source tracking>>=
function stateFor(element) {
    let state = states.get(element);
    if (!state) {
        state = { container: ++nextContainer, attempt: 0 };
        states.set(element, state);
    }
    if (state.markup !== element.innerHTML) {
        state.source = element.textContent || '';
        state.markup = element.innerHTML;
        state.completedTheme = undefined;
    }
    return state;
}
```

After an asynchronous render, verify that the container, its contents and the
current theme still match the work that was started.

```js : <<Mermaid preview source tracking>>=+
function isCurrent(element, state, markup, currentTheme) {
    return element.isConnected && element.matches(selector)
        && element.innerHTML === markup && state.markup === markup
        && theme() === currentTheme;
}
```

Report failures through textContent, including the original source for
inspection. An alert role and accessible label identify the rendering error
without treating either the error message or source as HTML.

```js : <<Mermaid preview source tracking>>=+
function showError(element, source, error) {
    const message = error instanceof Error ? error.message : String(error);
    element.textContent = `Mermaid diagram could not be rendered: ${message}\n\n${source}`;
    element.setAttribute('role', 'alert');
    element.setAttribute('aria-label', 'Mermaid diagram rendering error');
}
```

Every initialize/render pair runs to completion before the next initialization.
Theme or content events during an await request another pass. A stale result is
never installed into a detached, edited or differently themed container. We do
not invoke bindFunctions: interactive callbacks are unnecessary for this strict
preview. Mermaid performs its own temporary-render DOM cleanup.

```js : <<Mermaid preview rendering>>=
async function renderElement(element, currentTheme) {
    const state = stateFor(element);
    if (state.completedTheme === currentTheme) { return; }
    const markup = element.innerHTML;
    let result;
    let failure;
    try {
        validateSource(state.source);
        mermaid.initialize(configuration(currentTheme));
        result = await mermaid.render(diagramId(state), state.source);
    } catch (error) {
        failure = error;
    }
    if (!isCurrent(element, state, markup, currentTheme)) { return; }
    if (result) {
        element.innerHTML = result.svg;
        element.removeAttribute('role');
        element.removeAttribute('aria-label');
    } else {
        showError(element, state.source, failure);
    }
    state.markup = element.innerHTML;
    state.completedTheme = currentTheme;
}
```

The queue coalesces bursts without losing updates, including those delivered
while Mermaid is awaiting layout. The theme is sampled before each diagram,
not once for the whole document. The configuration key is the resolved catalogue
name, so even two palettes with the same light/dark mode trigger a fresh render.

```js : <<Mermaid preview scheduling>>=
async function drain() {
    running = true;
    try {
        while (pending) {
            pending = false;
            for (const element of document.querySelectorAll(selector)) {
                if (element.isConnected) { await renderElement(element, theme()); }
            }
        }
    } finally {
        running = false;
    }
}

```

Every update requests a pass, but only an idle queue starts a new drain. Events
arriving during a render are handled by the existing drain's next iteration.

```js : <<Mermaid preview scheduling>>=+
function schedule() {
    applyTheme();
    pending = true;
    if (!running) { void drain(); }
}
```

VS Code dispatches its content-update event on window, including replacement
documents. Observe theme attributes in the body subtree, not body classes or
Mermaid's own SVG writes. Waiting for DOMContentLoaded also supports scripts
loaded before the body exists.

```js : <<Mermaid preview startup>>=
function start() {
    const observer = new MutationObserver(schedule);
    observer.observe(document.body, {
        attributes: true, subtree: true, attributeFilter: ['data-literate-theme']
    });
    window.addEventListener('vscode.markdown.updateContent', schedule);
    schedule();
}

if (document.readyState === 'loading') {
    document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', start, { once: true });
} else {
    start();
}
```
